---
description: Building a RustStream service without the `macros` feature (hand-written handlers, registration and main)
globs:
alwaysApply: false
---

- Everything the attribute macros produce can be written by hand. `macros` is a convenience
  feature, not a layer: it expands to calls on the same public runtime API, so a service without it
  loses no capability that does not come *from* a declaration.
- What disappears with the feature off: `#[subscriber]`, `#[ruststream::app]`, and the
  `Message` / `Outgoing` / `OutSlot` / `OutMessages` / `FromRef` derives. Everything those expand
  into - extractor parameters (`State`, `Ctx`, `Headers`, `Out`, `Seek`), the reply clauses
  (`publish(..)`, `publish_raw(..)`), the mount-site settings builder (`.name`, `.workers`,
  `.on_failure`, `.buffered`) and `include` itself - goes with them, because all of it is carried
  by the *definition* the attribute mints.
- `use ruststream::prelude::*;` still works: the glob's macro re-exports are themselves
  feature-gated, so the rest of it (builder, `Context`, `HandlerResult`, `Name`, `Publisher`,
  `PublishExt`, `TypedPublisher`) comes in unchanged.
- A handler is any `Fn(&M, &mut Context<'_, C, S>) -> impl Future`, where the output is anything
  `Into<Settle>` (`HandlerResult`, `()`, `Result<_, E>`, `Settle`). Closures qualify, so do plain
  functions and any type implementing `Handler`.
- Register with `b.subscribe(source, handler, metadata)` (the portable form: the source resolves at
  startup) or `b.handle(subscriber, handler, metadata)` when you already hold an open subscriber.
  Routers have the same `subscribe` method, so grouping still works.
- Write `main` yourself: `#[tokio::main]` plus `app.run().await`. The attribute's CLI (`run`,
  `asyncapi gen`) is what you give up; `run_until(..)` and `start()` are plain methods.
- State is reached through `ctx.state()`, headers through `ctx.headers()`, and publishing through a
  publisher the closure captured or that lives in the state - the same objects the extractors would
  have handed over.

## A whole service, macro-free

```rust
use std::error::Error;

use ruststream::codec::JsonCodec;
use ruststream::memory::MemoryBroker;
use ruststream::prelude::*;
use ruststream::runtime::{HandlerMetadata, typed}; // outside the prelude
use serde::Deserialize;

#[derive(Debug, Deserialize)]
struct Order {
    id: u64,
}

fn app() -> RustStream {
    RustStream::new(AppInfo::new("orders", "0.1.0")).with_broker(MemoryBroker::new(), |b| {
        b.subscribe(
            Name::new("orders"),
            typed(JsonCodec, |order: &Order, _ctx: &mut Context| {
                let id = order.id;
                async move {
                    tracing::info!(id, "processing order");
                    HandlerResult::ack()
                }
            }),
            HandlerMetadata::typed::<Order>("orders"),
        );
    })
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn Error>> {
    app().run().await?;
    Ok(())
}
```

`run()` runs until `SIGINT` / `SIGTERM`; `run_until(future)` drives shutdown from a future you own
(a test signal, a timeout), and `start()` hands back a `RunningApp` for hosting the service beside
another foreground server.

## Decoding

`typed(codec, handler)` is what the attribute wraps a typed handler in: it decodes the delivery
with `codec` and calls the inner handler with `&T`. Name the codec explicitly - there is no
declaration site for a scope codec to reach.

```rust
use ruststream::runtime::{FailurePolicy, typed};

// the decode-failure policy, the manual counterpart of `on_failure(decode = ..)`
let handler = typed(JsonCodec, |order: &Order, _ctx: &mut Context| async move {
    HandlerResult::ack()
})
.on_decode_failure(FailurePolicy::Retry);
```

## Raw bytes

Without `typed`, the handler is called with the broker's own message, so the payload comes off it
directly and no codec is involved. This is the shape `#[subscriber(.., raw)]` produces, and the
only subscriber form available with no codec feature at all.

```rust
b.subscribe(
    Name::new("frames"),
    |msg: &MemoryMessage, _ctx: &mut Context| {
        let frame = msg.payload().to_vec();
        async move {
            parse(&frame);
            HandlerResult::ack()
        }
    },
    HandlerMetadata::raw("frames"),
);
```

Use `b.handle(subscriber, handler, meta)` instead when the broker hands you an open subscriber
before registration (how you obtain one is broker-specific; the in-memory broker's `subscribe` is
a plain synchronous call):

```rust
let subscriber = b.broker().subscribe("orders");
b.handle(subscriber, handler, HandlerMetadata::raw("orders"));
```

## State

There is no `State<T>` parameter and no `FromRef` derive to make one resolve. `on_startup` is
unchanged, and the handler reads the state off the context:

```rust
struct AppState {
    receipts: MemoryPublisher,
}

let app = RustStream::new(AppInfo::new("orders", "0.1.0"))
    .on_startup(async move |()| Ok::<_, std::convert::Infallible>(AppState { receipts }))
    .with_broker(MemoryBroker::new(), |b| {
        b.subscribe(
            Name::new("orders"),
            typed(JsonCodec, |order: &Order, ctx: &mut Context<'_, (), AppState>| {
                let payload = serde_json::to_vec(&Receipt { order_id: order.id }).expect("encode");
                let publisher = ctx.state().receipts.clone();
                async move {
                    if publisher.raw(&payload).to("receipts").publish().await.is_err() {
                        return HandlerResult::retry();
                    }
                    HandlerResult::ack()
                }
            }),
            HandlerMetadata::typed::<Order>("orders"),
        );
    });
```

The context's type parameters are what the attribute would have inferred: `Context<'_, C, S>` with
`C` the broker's per-delivery context and `S` the application state. Spell them out when the
closure needs the state; `Context<'_>` is the stateless default.

## Headers

`Headers<T>` is an extractor, so the manual path parses the map itself. It is the same call the
extractor makes, and it returns a `Result` you settle on yourself:

```rust
let Ok(meta) = ctx.headers().to_typed::<ChunkMeta>() else {
    return HandlerResult::drop();
};
```

`ctx.headers_mut()` is the working copy middleware enriches, and `HeaderMap::insert_typed(&value)`
writes a contract back into it.

## Publishing

`message(&value)` needs `T: OutgoingDestination`, which is what `#[derive(Outgoing)]` implements -
so without the derive the typed publish builder has nothing to resolve a destination from. Two
routes remain, and both are the ones the builder itself ends in:

```rust
// encode with the codec, then send the bytes to a destination you name
let bytes = JsonCodec.encode(&receipt)?;
publisher.raw(&bytes).to("receipts").publish().await?;

// or build the message and call the Publisher trait method directly
publisher
    .publish(OutgoingMessage::new("receipts", bytes.as_ref()).with_headers(headers))
    .await?;
```

`TypedPublisher`, `after_startup`, publish transforms and `publish_layer` are all runtime API and
work unchanged; only the `Out` slot *parameter* and the reply clauses are gone, so a handler that
publishes captures its publisher or reads it from the state.

## What has no macro-free equivalent

These are declarations, not behaviour, so there is nothing to hand-write:

- The AsyncAPI payload and headers schemas the derives contribute. `HandlerMetadata` still records
  the subscription name and the input type name, and `with_description` and the other builder
  setters fill in the rest by hand.
- The `Out` slot dictionary (`#[publishes(..)]`) and the message-set enums: they exist to be
  checked at compile time against a declaration site the manual path does not have.
- The mount-site settings builder. `b.subscribe` / `b.handle` register with the default failure
  policies; the decode policy is set on the `typed` wrapper instead.

If a service wants any of that, enable `macros` - it is additive, and the two styles compose in one
application: a macro-free registration and an `include(def)` can sit in the same `with_broker`
scope.
